## Problem

Design a two-player chess game in Java. Explain the classes, how a move is validated and committed, how special moves work, and how the game detects checkmate or stalemate. Support undo without corrupting the position.

## Requirements

- Standard 8×8 chess, two players, White first, all six piece types and captures.
- Castling, en passant and all four promotion choices.
- Reject moves out of turn, through blocking pieces, or leaving the moving side's king in check.
- Detect check, checkmate and stalemate; support resignation and an agreed draw.
- Retain enough history to undo an accepted action exactly.
- One game engine in one JVM. UI, matchmaking, computer search, clocks and tournament draw adjudication are separate extensions.

## 1. The answer I would present

I would make `Game` the only mutable entry point. It owns the current `Position`, result, monotonically increasing version and undo frames. The rules engine proposes a new immutable position; it never partially edits the live game. A successful command replaces the position and records its predecessor. An illegal command changes nothing.

My most important separation is **movement geometry versus legal movement**. A rook can travel horizontally or vertically until it meets a blocker. That describes a candidate move. Whether moving that rook exposes its king depends on the entire resulting position, so legality belongs to the rules engine, not to an isolated rook object.

The operation is therefore: validate whose turn it is, validate the move's geometry and special preconditions, apply every effect to a trial position, inspect the moving side's king, and only then commit. This same trial operation is used to enumerate legal replies and detect mate. I do not write a second, subtly different implementation for “checking whether a move would work.”

I choose a 64-cell array and immutable `Piece(side, kind)` values. It is easy to inspect, inexpensive for a human-versus-human engine, and straightforward to copy. I choose a `RuleSet` interface with a standard-chess implementation, rather than six mutable piece subclasses that also carry turn and history state. Piece-specific behaviour still exists; it is organized by kind inside the rule component. A variant replaces or extends that component without changing command sequencing in `Game`.

This answer implements movement, core end conditions, resignation and agreement. Threefold/fivefold repetition, fifty/seventy-five-move adjudication and dead-position detection are not silently assumed to work: the position retains the necessary counters, but a complete tournament adjudicator is additional work. That distinction makes the scope precise.

## 2. The object model and its invariants

| Type | Information and responsibility | Mutation rule |
|---|---|---|
| `Square` | Validated zero-based file/rank; converts `e4` to `(4,3)` | Immutable value |
| `Piece` | Side and kind; no independently mutable square or `hasMoved` flag | Immutable value |
| `Move` | Source, destination, optional promotion kind | A request, not proof of legality |
| `Position` | Board, turn, castling rights, en-passant target and counters | Immutable snapshot; board array never escapes |
| `RuleSet` | Legal-move enumeration, applying a legal move, check detection | Stateless policy |
| `StandardRules` | Standard movement, attacks, special moves and trial application | Produces a new position |
| `Game` | Current snapshot, version, result and undo frames | Commands serialize here |
| `Frame` | Position and result before an accepted action | Retained only in this game's history |
| `Game.View` | Snapshot returned to the caller, including current check status | Cannot mutate the engine |

I store a piece's location in the board, not both in the board and in a mutable `Piece.position`. Otherwise moving the board entry and forgetting the piece field creates two conflicting answers to “where is this rook?” Immutability also makes a shallow copy of the array sufficient: sharing a `Piece` value cannot let a trial move alter the live game.

The position includes more than visible pieces. A king and rook that moved away and returned look unchanged on the board, but castling is no longer permitted. Two identical boards with different turns or en-passant rights can have different legal moves. These values travel together in `Position`, including during undo.

[![The board after e2-e4 has Black to move, en-passant target e3, retained castling rights and updated counters; these are one position snapshot.](lld-figures/chess-position.svg)](lld-figures/chess-position.svg)

*A move changes one complete position. Restoring only the squares is not enough to restore the rules.*

The invariants are concrete: exactly one king per side; only the side to move can act; no accepted move leaves that side's king attacked; promotion cannot create a king or pawn; and a rejected action preserves board, rights, counters, history and version. Kings are not captured to signal victory. Checkmate is detected before any such capture could occur.

These are the value types from the executable Java 21 implementation:

```java
// source: Chess.java#values
    public enum Side { WHITE, BLACK;
        public Side other() { return this == WHITE ? BLACK : WHITE; }
        int step() { return this == WHITE ? 1 : -1; }
    }
    public enum Kind { KING, QUEEN, ROOK, BISHOP, KNIGHT, PAWN }
    public record Piece(Side side, Kind kind) {
        public Piece { Objects.requireNonNull(side); Objects.requireNonNull(kind); }
    }
    public record Square(int file, int rank) {
        public Square {
            if (file < 0 || file > 7 || rank < 0 || rank > 7)
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Square outside board");
        }
        public static Square at(String s) {
            if (s == null || !s.matches("[a-h][1-8]")) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Bad square");
            return new Square(s.charAt(0) - 'a', s.charAt(1) - '1');
        }
        int index() { return rank * 8 + file; }
        @Override public String toString() { return "" + (char)('a' + file) + (rank + 1); }
    }
    public record Move(Square from, Square to, Kind promotion) {
        public Move { Objects.requireNonNull(from); Objects.requireNonNull(to); }
        public static Move of(String from, String to) { return new Move(Square.at(from), Square.at(to), null); }
        public static Move promote(String from, String to, Kind kind) { return new Move(Square.at(from), Square.at(to), kind); }
    }
    public enum Result { ACTIVE, WHITE_WINS, BLACK_WINS, STALEMATE, AGREED_DRAW }
```

The `Side` argument to a game command represents an identity already resolved by the calling application. An eventual HTTP endpoint must map its authenticated player to that side; letting a browser simply claim `WHITE` would not be authorization.

## 3. Final design and the public contract

[![Game owns the current Position and undo frames, delegates rules through RuleSet, and returns immutable views; StandardRules implements the policy.](designs/lld-1.svg)](designs/lld-1.svg)

[![Detailed Java responsibilities: Game serializes commands, RuleSet separates standard rules, Position stores immutable board state, and Move and Piece are values.](lld-figures/chess-domain.svg)](lld-figures/chess-domain.svg)

My public commands are `move(actor, expectedVersion, move)`, `undo(expectedVersion)`, `resign(actor, expectedVersion)` and `agreeDraw(consentingSides, expectedVersion)`. Each returns a `Game.View`. A version conflict, terminal game and illegal move are distinguishable failures. In this local engine the Java exception types separate stale/terminal state from invalid input; an API adapter could map them to typed error codes.

`Position.at(square)` exposes an immutable piece or empty square. It does not expose the backing array or a mutable collection. The code's FEN parser is for fixtures/imports: it validates representation and king presence, but does not prove that an arbitrary imported position is historically reachable. Ordinary gameplay starts from `Position.initial()` and advances only through the rules engine.

I serialize a command on the `Game` instance, not on a static global monitor. Two separate games should proceed independently. The snapshot can be read safely after the lock is released because it cannot subsequently be mutated.

## 4. From a move request to a committed answer

### A normal move, with every state change accounted for

For White's `e2-e4` at version 0:

1. `Game` verifies ACTIVE, version 0 and White's turn.
2. The rules engine finds a White pawn on e2, confirms e4 is empty, and checks e3 is empty for the two-square advance.
3. It copies the board, clears e2 and places the pawn on e4.
4. The new position has Black to move and en-passant target e3. A pawn move resets the halfmove counter; the fullmove number stays 1 until Black completes a move.
5. White's king is tested in the resulting position. If safe, Black's legal replies determine the new result.
6. `Game` pushes the old position/result, commits the new ones and advances the version to 1.

If the same client submits a second command still expecting version 0, it loses the version check. The engine does not apply a second move to the old observation. Request-ID deduplication for a network retry can be added in the application layer; a version check alone rejects stale work rather than returning an earlier response.

### Why an attack map is a separate operation

For a rook or bishop, an attack ray stops at the first occupied square. A pawn attacks its forward diagonals even when they are empty; it does not attack the square straight ahead. A king attacks its adjacent squares. These questions are answered without recursively asking whether the attacking piece could make a fully legal move.

A pinned enemy piece is still relevant to king safety. If attack detection called legal-move generation, legal generation would call attack detection again, producing circular reasoning or incorrectly allowing a king onto a protected square. The engine implements attack geometry directly:

```java
// source: Chess.java#attacks
        public boolean attacked(Position p, Square target, Side by) {
            for (int i=0;i<64;i++) {
                Piece piece=p.board[i]; if (piece==null || piece.side!=by) continue;
                Square from=square(i); int dx=target.file-from.file, dy=target.rank-from.rank;
                boolean attacks=switch(piece.kind) {
                    case PAWN -> Math.abs(dx)==1 && dy==by.step();
                    case KNIGHT -> Math.abs(dx)*Math.abs(dy)==2;
                    case KING -> Math.max(Math.abs(dx),Math.abs(dy))==1;
                    case ROOK -> (dx==0 || dy==0) && pathClear(p,from,target);
                    case BISHOP -> Math.abs(dx)==Math.abs(dy) && pathClear(p,from,target);
                    case QUEEN -> (dx==0 || dy==0 || Math.abs(dx)==Math.abs(dy)) && pathClear(p,from,target);
                };
                if (!from.equals(target) && attacks) return true;
            }
            return false;
        }
```

To construct legal moves, the engine first applies geometry and occupancy checks, then applies the complete trial and tests king safety. The code below is shared by user moves and mate detection:

```java
// source: Chess.java#legal
        @Override public Position play(Position before, Move move) {
            if (!pseudoLegal(before, move)) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Illegal geometry or precondition");
            Position trial = applyUnchecked(before, move);
            if (inCheck(trial, before.turn)) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Own king exposed");
            return trial;
        }
        @Override public List<Move> legalMoves(Position p) {
            List<Move> legal = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int i = 0; i < 64; i++) {
                Piece piece = p.board[i];
                if (piece == null || piece.side != p.turn) continue;
                Square from = square(i);
                for (int j = 0; j < 64; j++) {
                    Square to = square(j);
                    Kind[] choices = piece.kind == Kind.PAWN && (to.rank == 0 || to.rank == 7)
                        ? new Kind[]{Kind.QUEEN, Kind.ROOK, Kind.BISHOP, Kind.KNIGHT} : new Kind[]{null};
                    for (Kind promotion : choices) {
                        Move move = new Move(from, to, promotion);
                        if (pseudoLegal(p, move) && !inCheck(applyUnchecked(p, move), p.turn)) legal.add(move);
                    }
                }
            }
            return List.copyOf(legal);
        }
        @Override public boolean inCheck(Position p, Side side) { return attacked(p, king(p, side), side.other()); }
```

The straightforward enumerator considers destinations for each owned piece. That is deliberately readable rather than an optimized search-engine move generator. For a fixed 64-square board it is acceptable; if generalized to B squares, the brute-force candidate pairs plus attack/path checks are materially more expensive than a constant-time lookup. I would not promise engine-search throughput from this representation.

### A legal-looking rook move that must be rejected

Put the White king on e1, a White rook on e2, the Black rook on e8 and the Black king on a8. `Re2-f2` is geometrically valid and the destination is empty. But the trial opens the e-file from the Black rook to White's king. The move is rejected, the turn remains White and the game version stays unchanged.

[![Before, the White rook on e2 blocks the Black rook on e8; after the proposed move to f2, the e-file reaches the White king on e1, so the trial is rejected.](lld-figures/chess-pin.svg)](lld-figures/chess-pin.svg)

*The right-hand board is a rejected trial, not the next live position. This is the difference between candidate movement and legal movement.*

## 5. Special moves, results and exact recovery

### Castling is a coordinated king-and-rook move

For White's kingside castle I require the king on e1, the correct White rook on h1, the kingside right still present and f1/g1 empty. The king cannot start in check, cross an attacked f1 or finish on attacked g1. The trial moves the king to g1 and rook to f1 together. Queenside similarly checks the path and relevant rights; b1 must be empty for the rook's path, although the king does not cross it.

I test the transit square using a position where the king has actually left e1. Reusing only the original board can miss an attack line uncovered by that departure. Destination safety is tested after the full castle, including the rook move.

[![A Black rook attacks f1 down the f-file: e1 and g1 can be safe while crossing f1 makes White's kingside castle illegal.](lld-figures/chess-castling.svg)](lld-figures/chess-castling.svg)

Rights are persistent historical facts. Moving a king clears both rights. Moving an original rook clears that side's corresponding right, and capturing a rook on its original square clears the associated right. Returning a rook to h1 does not recreate permission. Tracking only the current placement would incorrectly allow it.

### En passant changes a third square

The en-passant target exists only immediately after a two-square pawn advance. A diagonal pawn move into that empty target must find the opposing pawn on the adjacent rank. Applying the move removes that pawn, even though it is not on the destination square. Every other accepted move clears the old target.

The difficult case is a horizontal pin: White king h5, White pawn g5, Black pawn f5 and Black rook a5. Black has just advanced f7-f5. A trial `g5xf6 e.p.` clears both g5 and f5, exposing the rook's entire fifth-rank line to h5. Leaving the captured pawn on f5 during validation would wrongly hide the attack.

[![En passant from g5 to f6 removes the pawn on f5 as well as vacating g5; that opens the fifth rank from Black rook a5 to White king h5, making the capture illegal.](lld-figures/chess-en-passant.svg)](lld-figures/chess-en-passant.svg)

### Promotion is part of this move, not a later edit

A pawn reaching the last rank must choose queen, rook, bishop or knight. I reject a missing choice and reject KING/PAWN. Legal-move generation produces all four variants, because underpromotion can change check, stalemate or a tactical result. Capturing on the promotion square and replacing the pawn happen in the same trial.

These effects share one application function. Notice that counters and castling rights are updated here, so legality trials, actual moves and history cannot disagree about what the move means:

```java
// source: Chess.java#apply
        private Position applyUnchecked(Position p, Move m) {
            Piece[] b=p.board.clone(); Piece moving=p.at(m.from), captured=p.at(m.to);
            boolean pawn=moving.kind==Kind.PAWN;
            boolean ep=pawn && m.from.file!=m.to.file && captured==null;
            if (ep) { Square capture=new Square(m.to.file,m.from.rank);captured=b[capture.index()];b[capture.index()]=null; }
            b[m.from.index()]=null;
            b[m.to.index()]=m.promotion==null?moving:new Piece(moving.side,m.promotion);
            if (moving.kind==Kind.KING && Math.abs(m.to.file-m.from.file)==2) {
                int rookFrom=m.to.file==6?7:0, rookTo=m.to.file==6?5:3;
                b[new Square(rookTo,m.from.rank).index()]=b[new Square(rookFrom,m.from.rank).index()];
                b[new Square(rookFrom,m.from.rank).index()]=null;
            }
            int rights=p.castlingRights;
            if (moving.kind==Kind.KING) rights &= ~(right(moving.side,true)|right(moving.side,false));
            if (moving.kind==Kind.ROOK) rights=clearRookRight(rights,m.from,moving.side);
            if (captured!=null && captured.kind==Kind.ROOK) rights=clearRookRight(rights,m.to,captured.side);
            Square epTarget=pawn && Math.abs(m.to.rank-m.from.rank)==2
                ? new Square(m.from.file,(m.from.rank+m.to.rank)/2):null;
            return new Position(b,p.turn.other(),rights,epTarget,
                pawn||captured!=null?0:p.halfmoveClock+1,p.fullmoveNumber+(p.turn==Side.BLACK?1:0));
        }
```

### Checkmate and stalemate use the same legal-reply search

After an accepted move, the opponent is now the side to move. If it has a legal reply, the game remains ACTIVE, with `check` exposed separately in the view. With no legal replies, an attacked king means checkmate; an unattacked king means stalemate. “No moves” alone does not distinguish them.

For example, `f2-f3, e7-e5, g2-g4, Qd8-h4` leaves White in check with no legal reply: Black wins. In the fixture Black king a8, White queen c7 and White king c6, with Black to move, Black has no legal move but is not attacked: stalemate. These two cases are executable tests, not just names in a result enum.

### Undo restores a snapshot but never rewinds concurrency identity

I choose a stack of immutable before-positions/results. An undo restores the board, turn, rights, en-passant target and counters in one assignment. It also restores ACTIVE when undoing a mating move. That is simpler than reverse-engineering every special move.

The version still increases on undo. If a position from version 0 reappears at version 2, an old version-0 command must not become valid again. This prevents an ABA-style stale-command bug even though the board looks identical.

```java
// source: Chess.java#game
        public synchronized View move(Side actor,long expectedVersion,Move move) {
            requireActive(expectedVersion);
            if (actor!=position.turn) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Wrong side to move");
            Position next=rules.play(position,move); // May fail; no live state has changed.
            Result nextResult=evaluate(next);
            history.push(new Frame(position,result));
            position=next;result=nextResult;version++;
            return view();
        }
        public synchronized View undo(long expectedVersion) {
            if (expectedVersion!=version) throw new IllegalStateException("Stale version");
            if (history.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalStateException("No history");
            Frame before=history.pop();position=before.position;result=before.result;
            version++; // Monotonic: an old command must not become valid again after undo.
            return view();
        }
        private Result evaluate(Position p) {
            if (!rules.legalMoves(p).isEmpty()) return Result.ACTIVE;
            if (!rules.inCheck(p,p.turn)) return Result.STALEMATE;
            return p.turn==Side.WHITE?Result.BLACK_WINS:Result.WHITE_WINS;
        }
```

A make/unmake delta is an alternative when implementing deep search, but it must retain every overwritten value, captured square, promotion origin and castling rook movement. I would adopt it only with exact restoration tests and a measured need. An interface for every noun would not improve this correctness boundary.

### What the runnable implementation actually establishes

The [complete Chess.java](reference-code/lld-three/Chess.java) and [executable checks](reference-code/lld-three/ThreeChecks.java) need only Java 21. They include the rule engine above, game commands and immutable views. The tested scope deliberately excludes clocks and full tournament draw adjudication.

| Scenario | Expected result |
|---|---|
| Initial position, one/two/three plies | 20 / 400 / 8,902 legal move sequences |
| Blocked rook, wrong turn or stale version | Rejected without changing the live position |
| Pinned rook moves sideways | Rejected because the king becomes attacked |
| King moves into a pinned knight's attack | Rejected; attack semantics are not legal-move semantics |
| Castle through f1 under attack | Rejected even if g1 is safe |
| En passant exposes the fifth rank | Rejected after removing both relevant pawns |
| Pawn reaches last rank | Exactly four promotion choices; underpromotion retained |
| Fool's mate versus stalemate fixture | Distinct winner/stalemate outcomes |
| Undo a castle or checkmate | Entire position restored; version increases |

The move-tree counts are useful regression checks because they exercise many combinations, but they are not proof of every chess rule. The special fixtures target cases those opening counts do not cover.

## 6. Interviewer follow-ups

**Why not put `canMove` on each piece subclass?** That is a valid geometry organization. It does not remove the need for whole-position king-safety validation. I chose immutable kind/side values plus a rules component to avoid mutable inheritance state and make copying reliable. A polymorphic geometry strategy could be substituted without moving turn/history ownership out of Game.

**Is check a separate terminal state?** No. A checked player may have many legal replies. I expose it as a property of an ACTIVE position; only checkmate or another final result prevents further moves.

**How would I add clocks?** A `ClockPolicy` accounts for elapsed time when the owning game accepts a command and can produce a timeout result. It is not a field on individual pieces and it should not make pure movement tests sleep. I would define which event stops each player's clock and how a timeout races an arriving move under the same game command boundary.

**What changes with two servers?** The per-object monitor no longer covers all writers. I would persist a game version and conditionally update it in the authoritative store, or route that game to one elected owner with appropriate failover. The pure RuleSet/Position boundary stays useful; adding a concurrent map to each server would not establish global ordering.

**How would I replay a game?** Store the initial position/rules version and accepted commands, including promotion choices and final actions. Replay those commands, not just pairs of squares. Position snapshots accelerate recovery; command identities can let a network retry return its original result rather than create another action.

**What belongs in a repetition key?** Piece placement, side to move, castling rights and legally relevant en-passant availability affect equivalent play. Halfmove/fullmove counters serve other purposes. Simply serializing the board or including every incidental counter gives the wrong equivalence relation.

**Does a 64-cell copy waste memory?** Each retained position has a fixed-size array and small metadata. For a human game with H history entries, history is O(64H), which is reasonable for this scope. A search engine evaluating millions of nodes has a different workload; bitboards and reversible deltas become measured optimizations rather than compulsory interview decoration.

**How does this support variants?** Game depends on RuleSet. Chess960 changes castling layout/semantics and initialization inside an appropriate rules implementation; it should not require edits to undo, version checks or player identity. A variant with additional state may also require an extended position model—I would not promise every possible game variant fits the exact same fields.

## 7. Closing answer

“I would build an immutable position model and put all accepted changes behind a versioned Game. The rules component separates attacks from candidate movement, applies the complete trial—including special captures—and commits only when king safety holds. Legal replies give me checkmate and stalemate without a second rules implementation. Snapshots make undo exact, while a monotonic game version prevents stale commands from becoming valid again. That gives us a readable, tested engine before optimizing for search or adding network ownership.”

In a 45-minute round I would spend roughly five minutes fixing scope, eight on position ownership and class relationships, fifteen on the trial/commit code, ten on special moves and terminal cases, and the remainder defending undo and concurrency. The answer above supplies the details behind that presentation; the interview narrative is a prioritization of the finished solution.

